<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>In 711 Tariq ibn Ziyad, a commander probably of Berber origin serving the Umayyad governor of North Africa, Musa ibn Nusayr, crossed the strait into Visigothic Spain with an army of perhaps seven thousand men; the rock where he landed still bears his name, Jabal Tariq, Gibraltar. Arabic historians writing long afterwards gave him a speech to his troops as they prepared to face the army of King Roderic.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>O people, where can you flee? The sea is behind you and the enemy before you.</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">As given by Arabic historians writing centuries after the event, translated here; the famous text cannot be traced to Tariq himself.</p><div class="sp-full"><button type="button" class="sp-share" data-share-text="“O people, where can you flee? The sea is behind you and the enemy before you.” — Tariq ibn Ziyad, Tariq ibn Ziyad: The Sea Behind You" data-share-img="/images/og/speech-tariq-ibn-ziyad-the-sea-behind-you.jpg">Share this quote</button></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>Tariq's army destroyed Roderic's near the Guadalete in 711, and the Visigothic kingdom collapsed within a few years. Most of the Iberian Peninsula became al-Andalus, and Muslim rule lasted in part of it until the fall of Granada in 1492.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-conquest-of-hispania">Umayyad conquest of Hispania</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">Al-Andalus</a></div></div></section>
